1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,345
Aarav climbs the stairs at home.

2
00:00:02,345 --> 00:00:04,104
One light, but two switches.

3
00:00:04,404 --> 00:00:06,446
He flips the switch at the bottom.

4
00:00:06,446 --> 00:00:07,686
The light comes on.

5
00:00:07,986 --> 00:00:10,335
At the top, he flips the other switch.

6
00:00:10,335 --> 00:00:11,575
The light goes off.

7
00:00:11,875 --> 00:00:13,577
Either switch can change it.

8
00:00:13,577 --> 00:00:15,594
Which logic gate thinks like this?

9
00:00:16,094 --> 00:00:17,857
Meet the XOR gate.

10
00:00:17,857 --> 00:00:20,167
Its full name is exclusive OR.

11
00:00:20,517 --> 00:00:23,952
Exclusive means one or
the other, but not both.

12
00:00:24,302 --> 00:00:27,221
So the output is one when
the two inputs are different.

13
00:00:27,571 --> 00:00:31,583
The symbol is the OR shape, with
one extra curved line at the inputs.

14
00:00:32,233 --> 00:00:35,104
We write Y equals A, circle plus, B.

15
00:00:35,104 --> 00:00:37,334
The circled plus means XOR.

16
00:00:37,984 --> 00:00:40,595
Let's fill its truth
table on the breadboard.

17
00:00:40,945 --> 00:00:42,019
Both inputs zero.

18
00:00:42,019 --> 00:00:43,185
They are the same.

19
00:00:43,185 --> 00:00:44,393
The output is zero.

20
00:00:44,743 --> 00:00:45,777
Zero and one.

21
00:00:45,777 --> 00:00:46,677
Different.

22
00:00:46,552 --> 00:00:47,452
One.

23
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:48,073
One and zero.

24
00:00:48,073 --> 00:00:48,973
Different.

25
00:00:48,848 --> 00:00:49,748
One.

26
00:00:49,686 --> 00:00:50,805
Both inputs one.

27
00:00:50,805 --> 00:00:51,879
The same again.

28
00:00:51,879 --> 00:00:53,189
The output is zero.

29
00:00:53,539 --> 00:00:56,382
So XOR is one only when the inputs differ.

30
00:00:57,032 --> 00:00:58,347
Compare it with OR.

31
00:00:58,347 --> 00:01:00,467
They differ only in the last row.

32
00:01:01,117 --> 00:01:04,416
On the scope, Y goes high
only when A and B disagree.

33
00:01:05,066 --> 00:01:07,919
Now its partner, the XNOR gate.

34
00:01:07,919 --> 00:01:09,098
Exclusive NOR.

35
00:01:09,448 --> 00:01:12,395
It is an XOR gate with
a NOT gate after it.

36
00:01:12,745 --> 00:01:15,316
So its symbol gets a small
bubble at the output.

37
00:01:15,666 --> 00:01:16,566
Both zero.

38
00:01:16,475 --> 00:01:17,375
One.

39
00:01:16,949 --> 00:01:18,084
Different inputs.

40
00:01:18,084 --> 00:01:18,984
Zero.

41
00:01:18,604 --> 00:01:19,504
Both one.

42
00:01:19,367 --> 00:01:20,267
One.

43
00:01:20,191 --> 00:01:23,215
So XNOR is one when the
inputs are the same.

44
00:01:23,865 --> 00:01:25,713
It is a sameness checker.

45
00:01:25,713 --> 00:01:28,762
We write AXORB, with a bar on top.

46
00:01:29,412 --> 00:01:33,071
Now, the XOR formula,
built from basic gates.

47
00:01:33,421 --> 00:01:35,390
A one and B zero gives one.

48
00:01:35,390 --> 00:01:37,261
We write that as AB bar.

49
00:01:37,611 --> 00:01:40,038
A zero and B one also gives one.

50
00:01:40,038 --> 00:01:41,444
That is A bar B.

51
00:01:41,794 --> 00:01:43,348
Join them with OR.

52
00:01:43,348 --> 00:01:46,074
Y equals AB bar, plus A bar B.

53
00:01:46,724 --> 00:01:50,350
That needs two NOT gates, two
AND gates, and one OR gate.

54
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,601
The same job can also be done
with just four NAND gates.

55
00:01:55,251 --> 00:02:00,254
For XNOR, the same idea
gives AB, plus A bar B bar.

56
00:02:00,904 --> 00:02:02,277
Back to the stairs.

57
00:02:02,277 --> 00:02:03,992
Each switch is one input.

58
00:02:04,342 --> 00:02:07,817
Flip either switch, and the inputs
change between same and different.

59
00:02:08,167 --> 00:02:10,084
So the light flips each time.

60
00:02:10,084 --> 00:02:12,354
It works just like an XOR gate.

61
00:02:13,004 --> 00:02:14,985
XOR has two neat tricks.

62
00:02:15,335 --> 00:02:17,540
AXOR zero gives back A.

63
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:19,637
AXOR one gives A bar.

64
00:02:19,987 --> 00:02:22,688
So one input can turn a NOT on and off.

65
00:02:23,338 --> 00:02:27,472
And with many inputs, XOR gives
one when the number of ones is odd.

66
00:02:27,822 --> 00:02:31,285
Computers use this odd or even
check to spot errors in data.

67
00:02:31,935 --> 00:02:33,242
Different gives one.

68
00:02:33,242 --> 00:02:34,365
Same gives zero.

69
00:02:35,165 --> 00:02:36,151
Worked example.

70
00:02:36,151 --> 00:02:38,279
Find Y for this three gate circuit.

71
00:02:38,629 --> 00:02:41,394
The first NOR gate gives A plus B, barred.

72
00:02:41,394 --> 00:02:43,021
The AND gate gives AB.

73
00:02:43,721 --> 00:02:45,581
The last NOR joins them.

74
00:02:45,581 --> 00:02:47,495
One long bar covers both.

75
00:02:48,195 --> 00:02:50,764
Break the long bar and change the sign.

76
00:02:50,764 --> 00:02:52,644
The two bars on top cancel.

77
00:02:53,344 --> 00:02:56,117
We get A plus B, times AB barred.

78
00:02:56,117 --> 00:02:57,658
Break that bar too.

79
00:02:58,358 --> 00:02:59,332
Multiply out.

80
00:02:59,332 --> 00:03:01,766
AA bar and BB bar are both zero.

81
00:03:02,466 --> 00:03:05,215
Y equals AB bar, plus A bar B.

82
00:03:05,215 --> 00:03:07,672
The circuit is an XOR gate.

83
00:03:08,372 --> 00:03:09,979
Check A one, B one.

84
00:03:09,979 --> 00:03:11,164
NOR gives zero.

85
00:03:11,164 --> 00:03:12,300
AND gives one.

86
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:13,234
Y is zero.

87
00:03:13,884 --> 00:03:15,348
Now, the exam trap.

88
00:03:15,698 --> 00:03:18,839
Many students mix up XOR
and OR in the last row.

89
00:03:19,189 --> 00:03:21,154
With both inputs one, OR gives one.

90
00:03:21,504 --> 00:03:23,159
But XOR gives zero.

91
00:03:23,159 --> 00:03:26,450
The inputs are the same,
so the output is zero.

92
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:28,782
Where is this asked?

93
00:03:28,782 --> 00:03:33,049
In PUC board papers, and
in ISC Computer Science.

94
00:03:33,399 --> 00:03:36,717
JEE Main has asked
which gate a circuit is.

95
00:03:36,717 --> 00:03:38,460
The answer was XOR.

96
00:03:38,810 --> 00:03:43,484
Expect truth tables, the XOR formula,
and circuits that turn out to be

97
00:03:43,484 --> 00:03:44,384
XOR.

98
00:03:44,853 --> 00:03:45,753
Try this.

99
00:03:45,665 --> 00:03:48,091
A and one go into an XOR gate.

100
00:03:48,091 --> 00:03:51,330
That output and B go
into a second XOR gate.

101
00:03:51,330 --> 00:03:52,251
What is Y?

102
00:03:57,651 --> 00:03:58,551
B.

103
00:03:58,102 --> 00:03:59,430
AXNORB.

104
00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:01,691
XOR with one flips A.

105
00:04:01,691 --> 00:04:03,696
The first output is A bar.

106
00:04:04,046 --> 00:04:06,737
So Y is A bar, XORB.

107
00:04:07,087 --> 00:04:08,768
Test both inputs zero.

108
00:04:08,768 --> 00:04:09,974
A bar is one.

109
00:04:09,974 --> 00:04:11,846
One XOR zero is one.

110
00:04:12,196 --> 00:04:13,771
Same inputs give one.

111
00:04:13,771 --> 00:04:15,213
That is XNOR.

112
00:04:15,863 --> 00:04:17,409
Screenshot this card.

113
00:04:20,609 --> 00:04:23,271
Next, the half adder and the full adder.

114
00:04:23,271 --> 00:04:24,879
XOR does the adding.
